FOOD_EXTRA = {
 'chocolate': 'Baking chocolate and dark chocolate contain more theobromine per gram than milk chocolate. Wrappers, brownie batter, cocoa powder, mocha drinks and sugar-free chocolate that also contains xylitol all count as exposures. Seasoned or filled chocolates add fat and other ingredients but do not make theobromine safer. Cats are small; even a lick is still worth a professional call rather than a wait-and-see experiment.',
 'onions': 'Onion powder, onion salt, fried onions, soup bases and baby food with onion are easy to miss. Cooking, drying or powdering does not make Allium plants safe for cats. Compared with chives or garlic, the hazard theme is the same—red blood cell damage—while the form on a human plate often looks too small to matter.',
 'garlic': 'Garlic salt, garlic oil, garlic powder and savory broth concentrates are common hidden sources. Garlic is often described as more potent than onion on a weight basis among Allium foods, which is why leftover takeout and seasoned meats are frequent accidental exposures. Treat any intentional sharing of garlic-seasoned food as a vet call, not a home experiment.',
 'chives': 'Chives often appear as a garnish on eggs, baked potatoes and cream soups. The concern is the Allium family effect on red blood cells—the same theme as onion and garlic—even when the green flecks look too small to matter. Dried chives in spice jars still count. A cat that licks a chive-topped plate is an exposure worth a professional call, not a home dose chart.',
 'grapes': 'Because cat-specific toxicity data are limited, CatCalc does not invent a toxic dose for cats. We still advise against offering grapes and prompt veterinary contact after ingestion. Fresh fruit, chopped fruit cups and just-one-grape training treats are the usual household routes; dogs have a clearer kidney-injury record, and that uncertainty is not permission to feed cats.',
 'raisins': 'Raisin bread, trail mix and cereal clusters can combine raisins with chocolate, xylitol or macadamia ingredients. Dried fruit is easy to underestimate by volume. Bring the package to the call if you can. As with grapes, we do not invent a cat-specific toxic dose.',
 'alcohol': 'Uncooked yeast dough, some sauces, spilled cocktails and “a sip won’t hurt” experiments are common routes. Ethanol absorbs quickly; a cat’s small body reaches a dangerous dose far faster than a human’s. Hopped beer and cocktails with caffeine or xylitol add separate hazards. Treat any known drink as an emergency call, not a wait-and-see night.',
 'coffee': 'Coffee grounds in compost bins, espresso pucks, cold-brew concentrate, tea bags and chewable caffeine tablets pack more caffeine than a cold sip of milky coffee. Energy drinks and some pain relievers also contain caffeine. Restlessness, a racing heart, tremors and vomiting are reasons to call immediately—do not invent a “safe sip” volume.',
 'raw-dough': 'Raw yeast dough can expand in the stomach and ferment into alcohol. Both mechanisms are dangerous for a small animal. Pizza dough left to rise, bread machines and discarded scraps are common sources. Fully baked plain bread is a different (and still low-nutrition) question covered on the bread page.',
 'xylitol': 'Check peanut butter, syrups, gum, mints, dental products and some baked goods. Dog hypoglycemia talking points should not be pasted onto cats without noting the limited feline evidence. Mixed products may also contain chocolate or other hazards—read the full ingredient list for the hotline call.',
 'milk': "Lactose-free cat milk products exist as treats; they are still treats, not a hydration strategy. Growing orphans need commercial kitten milk replacer under proper guidance, never cow's milk as a substitute. Flavored coffee creamers and chocolate milk add caffeine or theobromine risks on top of lactose.",
 'cheese': 'Lactose is lower than in milk, but fat and salt are not free. Soft cheese, hard cheese and cheese sauces differ; sauces often hide onion or garlic. Medication hiding in cheese should be vet-approved for that drug and that cat. A pea-sized nibble is the usual outer bound people discuss—not a cheese board.',
 'yogurt': 'Fruit-on-the-bottom cups, chocolate swirls, honey toppings and sugar-free products with polyols are the usual traps. Plain, unsweetened yogurt with no xylitol is the only version worth discussing—and even then only as a tiny optional spoonful if your cat tolerates dairy. Yogurt is not a probiotic prescription; gut problems need a vet.',
 'tuna': 'A tuna-only diet can contribute to nutritional imbalance over time. Human canned tuna in brine is salty; oil-packed tuna adds fat. Fish bones in some products are a separate injury risk. Rotate complete cat foods as the staple; keep tuna as a high-value training bit in spring water when you use it at all.',
 'raw-fish': 'Some raw fish contain thiaminase, an enzyme that can destroy vitamin B1 (thiamine) when fed routinely. Raw fish also carries bacteria and parasite risks that home freezing does not reliably erase. Plain cooked, deboned fish as an occasional treat is a different page from a raw-fish diet experiment.',
 'raw-eggs': 'Raw eggs can carry Salmonella or E. coli. Raw egg white also contains avidin, which can interfere with biotin absorption when fed often. Fully cooking eggs addresses the main household-sharing risks; adding butter, salt, onion powder or chili oil afterward reintroduces other problems.',
 'bones': 'Cooked bones become brittle and can splinter, causing choking or injuries to the mouth, throat or gut. Raw bones still carry fracture and infection risks and are not a “safer leftover.” If a bone is stuck or the cat struggles to breathe, that is emergency care—not an internet search.',
 'dog-food': 'Shared households sometimes leave dog bowls accessible. One stolen kibble is usually not an emergency. Months of dog food as the only diet is a nutrition problem: cats need higher protein and nutrients such as taurine and arachidonic acid that dog formulas are not designed to supply long-term.',
 'ham': 'Breakfast meats and deli ham are very high in salt and fat. A stolen bite is unlikely to poison a healthy cat, but large or repeated servings can upset the stomach and, over time, contribute to pancreatitis risk and extra calories. Choose plain cooked lean meats instead of cured products.',
 'avocado': 'Severe persin toxicity is better documented in other species; cat-specific evidence is limited, so CatCalc does not invent a feline toxic dose. The pit can cause choking or obstruction, and the fatty flesh may cause digestive upset. Guacamole often adds onion, garlic and salt—read the dish, not just the fruit name.',
 'peanut-butter': 'Peanut butter is sticky, calorie-dense and sometimes sweetened with xylitol. Stickiness can lodge on the palate; fat can upset sensitive stomachs. At most a lick of a xylitol-free jar is the outer bound people discuss—and many cats do not need it at all.',
 'bread': 'Plain baked bread is mostly starch and offers cats almost no useful nutrition. Garlic bread, onion focaccia, raisin bread and chocolate-chip loaves each import separate hazards. Raw yeast dough is an emergency topic on its own page. A crumb of plain bread is optional novelty, not a meal.',
 'banana': 'Banana is not on classic toxin lists, but it is high in sugar and most cats cannot taste sweetness the way humans do. A thumbnail-sized piece of ripe flesh is the usual outer bound; peels are not food. Banana bread with xylitol, chocolate or raisins is a different exposure.',
 'apples': 'Apple flesh can be an optional tiny treat once the core, seeds and stems are removed. The ASPCA notes that stems, leaves and seeds contain cyanogenic compounds—so preparation matters more than the fruit’s reputation as “healthy.” Skip pies, sauces with xylitol and caramel coatings.',
 'chicken': 'Rotisserie seasonings, onion gravy, barbecue sauce and cooked bones undo the benefit of an otherwise suitable lean meat. Plain leftover breast or thigh meat, cooled and shredded, is the template. Raw chicken is a different question (pathogens)—this page is about cooked plain chicken as a small treat.',
 'turkey': 'Plain cooked turkey breast without skin, gravy, stuffing or bones can be a small high-protein treat. Holiday platters are where garlic, onions, raisin stuffing and cooked bones sneak in. Raw turkey has the same pathogen caveats as other raw poultry.',
 'salmon': 'Plain cooked salmon provides protein and omega-3 fatty acids, but fish should stay a treat relative to a complete cat diet. Remove all bones. Skip heavy seasoning, teriyaki glaze and restaurant butter sauces. Routine raw salmon is covered under raw-fish concerns, not this cooked-treat page.',
 'cooked-eggs': "Eggs are nutrient-dense; a whole egg may be too many calories for a small cat's treat budget. Share a bite, not a breakfast plate. Butter, milk, cheese, onion powder or chili oil in scrambled eggs reintroduce problems covered on other pages. Raw eggs are covered separately because of pathogen and avidin concerns.",
 'shrimp': 'Plain cooked shrimp with the shell, tail and digestive tract removed can be a high-value training bit. Garlic butter restaurant shrimp is not the same food. Shells are choking hazards; seasoned broths may hide onion or garlic powder.',
 'pumpkin': 'Plain cooked or plain canned pumpkin contains fiber; it is not a cure-all for unexplained constipation or diarrhea. Pie filling may contain sugar, spices, xylitol or other unsuitable ingredients. Persistent litter-box changes need a veterinarian, not endless pumpkin experiments.',
 'blueberries': 'Blueberries are generally treated as non-toxic and low-calorie. Many cats ignore them entirely, which is fine. Offer one or two berries, cut in half for small or gulping cats. Skip muffins, syrups and sugar-free products that may contain xylitol. Antioxidant marketing does not make blueberries a required feline food when a complete diet is already in place.',
 'watermelon': 'Seedless watermelon flesh is mostly water and sugar. Remove all seeds and the rind before offering a small cube. Rind is hard to chew and digest; seeded melon adds choking and intestinal risks. Watermelon is novelty—not a water-bowl replacement—and mixed fruit cups sometimes hide grapes, which are an avoid food on their own page.',
 'strawberries': 'Strawberries are generally treated as non-toxic for cats, though they are sugary and optional. Remove the leafy tops and offer only a small piece of flesh. Jam, syrup, yogurt blends and chocolate-covered strawberries import sugar, fat or other hazards that are not “just strawberry.” Complete cat food remains the staple; fruit is novelty inside the treat calorie budget, not a required vitamin strategy.',
 'cantaloupe': 'Some cats investigate cantaloupe’s aroma. The flesh can be an optional small treat once rind and seeds are removed. As with other melons, this is sugar and water rather than a balanced meal. Store fruit salads often include grapes or sugary syrup—inspect the mix before sharing anything from a human bowl, and keep portions inside treat calories.',
 'carrots': 'Soft, plain cooked carrot cut into small pieces can be an occasional treat. Hard raw chunks are a choking risk, especially for enthusiastic gulpers. Butter, honey glaze and onion soup mix turn a simple vegetable into a problem plate.',
 'cucumber': 'Cucumber is mostly water and novelty for cats that bother to try it. Peel if the skin is waxed; slice thin to reduce gulping. Pickled cucumber brings vinegar and salt; seasoned salads often include onion or garlic. Cucumber is not a hydration plan and not a diet food—complete cat food and fresh water remain the basics.',
 'green-beans': 'Plain cooked green beans are low in calories and generally safe. A few cats accept them as training bits. Butter, salt, garlic and fried onion toppings undo that. Canned beans in salted water should be rinsed or skipped.',
 'peas': 'Peas appear in many commercial cat-food recipes. Plain cooked or thawed frozen peas without seasoning can be a rare extra for cats that accept them. Butter, mint sauce, onion gravy and creamy casseroles turn peas into a different dish. Peas do not replace animal protein; keep any extras inside the treat calorie budget and a complete diet as the everyday staple.',
 'rice': 'Plain cooked white rice is sometimes used short-term in bland diets under veterinary direction. It is easy to digest starch, not a protein substitute, and should not crowd out a complete cat diet. Fried rice, garlic rice and onion pilaf are different foods.',
 'oatmeal': 'Plain oatmeal cooked thoroughly with water (not milk) can be a small optional fiber-containing treat for some cats. Instant flavored packets usually include sugar, salt or other additives. Sweeteners, butter, chocolate chips and sugar-free syrups (xylitol risk) do not belong in the pot. Unexplained constipation or diarrhea still needs a veterinary assessment rather than endless cereal trials.',
 'catnip': 'Catnip (Nepeta cataria) is non-toxic for cats. Roughly half to two-thirds of cats respond with playful behavior; response is partly genetic. Eating a large pile may cause vomiting or diarrhea. Toys should be sized so the cat cannot swallow string, plastic eyes or loose stuffing.',
}

